   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "Let \\( g(x) = \\int_{0}^{2 x} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\, dt \\). Find the exact value of \\( g'\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\)."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": 2
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "By the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, the derivative of the integral with respect to its upper limit is the integrand evaluated at that limit, \\( \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Sin",
            [
               "Multiply",
               2,
               "x"
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "The upper limit is \\( 2 x \\), so the chain rule multiplies by its derivative: \\( g'(x) = \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} \\cdot 2 \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Multiply",
            2,
            [
               "Sin",
               [
                  "Multiply",
                  2,
                  "x"
               ]
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "At \\( x = \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\) the limit is \\( \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\) and its derivative is \\( 2 \\).",
         "mathjson": 2
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "So \\( g'\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 1 \\cdot 2 = 2 \\).",
         "mathjson": 2
      }
   ],
